4.8.3.m:
They slay Soma when they press [him];
they slay the sacrifice.
When sacrificial gifts are given,
they invigorate the sacrifice.
That is the nature of sacrificial gifts (dakṣiṇā), that they invigorate (dakṣayanti).
The midday pressing is indeed the heavenly world.
When sacrificial gifts are given at the midday pressing,
[it is] for the ascending of the heavenly world.
Much should be given;
he makes a bridge
for the attainment of the heavenly world.
There is gold in his hand, and then he leads [them];
gold is truth;
by means of truth indeed he leads them.
In front of the Gārhapatya,
behind the Sadas,
they let them loose northward, between the pit and the Āgnīdhra hut.
By this [path] indeed the Aṅgirases went to the heavenly world;
along that path he leads them.
With two [verses] he offers in the Gārhapatya;
with that he steps upon this [earth].
In the Āgnīdhra with a verse to Agni;
with that, the intermediate space.
Then, when sacrificial gifts are given,
with that, the heavenly world.
He gives first to the Āgnīdhra;
the Āgnīdhra is the mouth of the sacrifice;
he does not go away from the mouth of the sacrifice.
Moreover, Agni is all deities;
the deities become sought and pleased for him by it.
He gives to the Brahman;
the Brahman belongs to Prajāpati;
he pleases Prajāpati with it.
He gives to the ṛtvijs;
he pleases the offerings with it.
With that which he gives to the sadasyas, he buys back his Soma-drinking.
With that which he gives to one descended from a ṛṣi and learned, he pleases the metres;
for they do not deserve that Soma-drinking should be bought back from him.
With that which he gives to one descended from a ṛṣi and learned, he prospers in the world of the gods;
with that [which he gives] to one not descended from a ṛṣi [yet] learned, in the world of men;
with that which he gives to one who has not crept [into the Sadas], trees spread;
with that which he gives to one who begs, he enlivens the rival;
with that [which he gives] through fear, the Brahman consumes royal power.
That which he thrusts away is a tigress sacrificial gift;
if he should receive it again, having become a tigress it would crush him;
it must be received together with another [gift];
thus it does not crush him.
When he gives a goat,
the goat belongs to Agni;
by it he reaches Agni's dear domain.
When he gives a sheep,
by it he wins cattle-wealth.
When he gives a cow,
the cow belongs to the Viśve Devas;
by it he reaches the dear domain of the Viśve Devas.
When he gives a garment,
a garment belongs to all deities;
by it the deities become sought and pleased for him.
When he gives prepared food,
by it he redeems flesh.
When a cart or a chariot,
by it the body.
When he gives gold,
by it he makes his life-span longer.
When he gives a horse,
the horse belongs to Prajāpati;
by it he reaches Prajāpati's dear domain;
moreover, he conquers the world of yonder Āditya.
At the end [a gift] must be given to the Pratihartṛ;
the Pratihartṛ belongs to Rudra;
if he should give to the Pratihartṛ in the middle, he would lead Rudra into the middle;
therefore [a gift] must be given to the Pratihartṛ at the end.
Svarbhānu the Āsura pierced the sun with darkness;
Atri perceived it.
When he gives gold to a descendant of Atri,
he smites away darkness;
moreover, he places light above,
for the illumination of the heavenly world.

4.8.4.m:
Viśvarūpa, son of Tvaṣṭṛ, thought that that part of the sacrifice was deficient
when the Ṛc and the Sāman are shifted apart.
He saw these [formulas],
for the repair of the sacrifice.
Whatever of the sacrifice is deficient here, these are offered for its repair.
There are six containing verses;
six, indeed, are the seasons;
he firmly stands in the seasons.
There are three yajus formulas;
three, indeed, are the pressings;
he obtains the pressings.
He offers these nine samiṣṭayajus formulas.
They praise with nine [verses] at the Bahiṣpavamāna;
that makes nine.
Nine are the life-breaths;
he places the life-breaths in himself.
Standing upright, he offers;
for standing upright, he is more vigorous.
For him whom he hates, he should offer standing silently;
he cuts off his life-breaths.
He offers continuously,
for the continuity of the life-breaths.
He offers without interruption,
so that the life-breaths are not interrupted.
If he were to interrupt, he would interrupt the life-breaths.
For him whom he hates, he should interrupt;
he interrupts his life-breaths.
With the same [breath] he offers dividing [the formulas];
for these life-breaths are equal.
These, indeed, are the linchpins of the sacrifice.
Viśvāmitra saw these two linchpins of the sacrifice;
therefore these are made the linchpins of the sacrifice.
'From where the sacrifice is taken, there it must be deposited again,' said Bharadvāja.
From where the sacrifice is taken, there indeed it is deposited again.

4.8.5.m:
The sacrifice indeed has five pressings:
the three pressings,
the concluding bath, and the anūbandhyā [cow] is the fifth of the pressings.
The sacrifice is fivefold;
as great as the sacrifice is, he seizes it.
Whatever exceeds the sacrifice, Varuṇa seizes that.
Exceeding the sacrifice is the pressed-out Soma sediment;
the pressing-board and the [other] pressing-board.
Therefore they go down to the concluding bath with it.
They go down to water;
for water is Varuṇa.
They go down to standing [water];
for that is visibly Varuṇa.
If they were to go down to flowing [water], they would carry away the cattle;
and when they go down to standing [water],
[it is] for the non-carrying-away of the cattle.
Water indeed is Varuṇa;
wind is Varuṇa.
If they were to go down to the concluding bath northward, Varuṇa would seize the offspring facing him.
And when they go down to the concluding bath southward,
[it is] for the sacrificer's freedom from Varuṇa.
'King Varuṇa has made a broad [path],' they say as they proceed from the cātvāla.
This indeed is the unobstructed, safe path by which yonder sun goes;
'for the path of yonder sun,' [he means].
'A hundred healers are yours, O king, a thousand,' they say, looking away at the water.
They adorn the water;
purified, they walk with these purified [waters].
There are [cakes] on one potsherd;
man is not to be obtained by potsherds;
he obtains him in a single way.
Moreover, this [earth] is one;
he firmly stands on this [earth].
Having thrown a blade of grass, he offers;
he offers on what has fire, what has an abode.
The Adhvaryu would become blind if he were to offer on what lacks an abode.
He offers the fore-offerings without the sacrificial grass;
the sacrificial grass is offspring;
he releases the offspring from death.
He offers the two butter-portions,
for the nature of the sacrifice.
He offers to Varuṇa,
for freedom from Varuṇa.
He offers to Agni and Varuṇa;
he frees him from Varuṇa on both sides.
He does not offer the after-offerings, nor do they bring [it] to completion;
for they consider that to be superfluous.
'In the ocean is your heart, within the waters,' he stirs [it] together with the ladle;
whatever cruel act is done here, he appeases it.
'O concluding bath, splash down,' [he says];
this is his concluding bath.
Then what pressed-out Soma sediment floats up, they consume that;
for they consider that to be like the drinking of Soma.
The pressed-out Soma sediment is Varuṇa.
If he were to consume [it], he would smear Varuṇa onto himself.
Regarding that, they say:
'It must be consumed;'
the pressed-out Soma sediment is cattle;
'he smears cattle onto himself.'
'Loosened is Varuṇa's noose,' [he says];
he loosens Varuṇa's noose.
'Cast back is Varuṇa's noose,' [he says];
he casts back Varuṇa's noose.
'Homage to Varuṇa's noose,' [he says];
he has paid homage to Varuṇa's noose.
When the Aṅgirases were rising up, the demons rose up after [them].
Bṛhaspati followed after them with this;
with it, he drove off the demons for them.
When he follows after with this,
[it is] for driving off the demons.
Because it has an upright stobha,
it thereby belongs to Bṛhaspati.
And because it has three stobhas:
three, indeed, are these worlds;
from these worlds, from the sacrifice, he drives off the demons.
Because [it is] in an atichandas metre:
all the metres are indeed the atichandas;
with all the metres he drives off the demons from the sacrifice.
Because [it is] addressed to Agni:
Agni indeed is all the deities;
with all the deities he drives off the demons from the sacrifice.
They go down to the water;
waters indeed are demon-slayers;
the demons do not cross the waters;
[it is] for the driving off of the demons.
Without looking back, they come,
so that Varuṇa does not follow after.
They wipe themselves outside the cowpen;
outside the cowpen they apportion Varuṇa away.
'You are fuel, may we prosper,' [he says];
having become free from Varuṇa, they approach in order to prosper.
'You are a kindling stick, may we be kindled,' [he says];
for being kindled.

4.8.7.m:
He offers with the Ṛc, he conducts with the Yajus,
he causes to go with the cup.
The Ṛc is for the stotra, the Yajus for the śastra.
Connected indeed are the stotra and the śastra;
connected are the Ṛc and the Yajus.
With the Ṛc it is performed loudly, with the Sāman loudly, with the Yajus in a low voice.
The gods thought:
'This part of the sacrifice is slack.'
They added the puroruc for non-slackness.
All deities expect, when a cup is being drawn:
'He is drawing for me, he is drawing for me.'
As for the puroruc,
[it is] for the distinction of the deities.
'Living are ye by name,
as such, make this one live;
Means of living are ye by name,
as such, make this one live;
Reviving are ye by name,
as such, revive this one.'
Having inserted twenty-one barley-grains and darbhapiñjūla stalks, he should address [them] thus.
Then he should consecrate [him with the formula]:
'May the Upāṃśu and the Antaryāma [cups] protect thine in-breath and out-breath;
may the Upāṃśusavana protect thy diffused breath;
may the Aindravāyava protect thy speech;
may the Maitrāvaruṇa protect thy ability and resolve;
may the Āśvina protect thy hearing;
may the Śukra and Manthin [cups] protect thine eyes;
may the Āgrāyaṇa protect thy self;
may the Ukthya protect thy limbs;
may the Dhruva protect thy life-span;
may the two season vessels protect thy breasts;
may the droṇakalaśa protect thy head;
may the two kalasa vessels protect thy flanks;
O Lord of nourishment, give back to him eye for eye, breath for breath, speech for speech, life-breath for life-breath; svāhā!'
Thus he should offer.
This indeed Vāsiṣṭha Sātyahavya used to say:
'I know that brāhmaṇa explanation regarding the vessels.
Just as a man is brought an offering once, only once should he do it, not before that.
If he should do it again, he would die.'
If he should practice sorcery, having uttered this very yajus, he should then consecrate [him]
again in the opposite way.

4.8.8.m:
Some vessels are employed again, others are not.
Those that are employed again are for this world;
those that are not employed again are for yonder dwelling.
Now this ukthya vessel is employed again.
It corresponds indeed to wild animals;
by this means he holds the wild animals.
Because this is employed again,
therefore they kill these [wild animals] while facing them, having surrounded them.
Now this season vessel is employed again.
It corresponds indeed to the horse;
by this means he holds the horse.
Because this is employed again,
therefore the hoof of this horse is beneath [it], as it were.
Now this Āditya vessel is employed again;
it corresponds indeed to the cow;
by this means he holds the cow.
Because the most Soma is drawn with the most verses,
therefore the cow, though being slaughtered and eaten, is the most numerous of domestic animals.
Now this Śukra vessel is employed again;
it corresponds indeed to man;
by this means he holds man.
Yonder sun is Śukra;
the rays are the seasons;
inner is the season vessel.
The Śukra vessel is employed again,
for the rays are inside from yonder sun.
Now this Upāṃśu vessel is employed again;
it corresponds indeed to the sheep;
by this means he holds the sheep.
Now this Antaryāma vessel is employed again;
it corresponds indeed to the goat;
by this means he holds the goat.
The Āgrāyaṇa is Prajāpati;
because this is employed again,
therefore Prajāpati knows [his] creatures.
The droṇakalaśa is this vessel of Prajāpati;
because this is employed again,
therefore this dwelling exists.
Seven vessels indeed are worthy of repeated employment,
for they have connections.
Seven metres;
the metres are speech;
as great as speech is, that he attains.
Seven metres,
seven Hotrās,
seven domestic animals:
these he obtains.
Three vessels indeed have direct connections;
everything else has indirect connections.
The Aindravāyava is the Gāyatrī,
the Śukra is the Triṣṭubh,
the Āgrāyaṇa is the Jagatī.
He should cause it to stream without interruption;
his wish becomes fulfilled;
and Parjanya becomes one who rains without clouds.
He should not lead [the cows] toward the cup.
If he were to lead [them] toward the cup, the offspring would die in the womb.
They should glide back by the very way they glide forward at first.
If they were to glide by another way, the embryos would be liable to slip out.
By the sacrificial post indeed the oblations go to the heavenly world;
the dakṣiṇās [go] by the Adhvaryu.
The sacrificial post is erected upright; standing upright he responds,
for the attainment of the heavenly world.

4.9.2.m:
Homage to Speech!
Homage to the Lord of Speech!
Homage to that Speech which is uttered, and which is not uttered!
Homage to the Ṛṣis, the mantra-makers, the mantra-knowers, the mantra-lords!
Let not the Ṛṣis, the mantra-makers, the mantra-knowers, forsake me!
May I utter divine Speech, pleasing to the gods, possessing svadhā for the Fathers, assented to by men;
let the gods aid that [speech] of mine for splendor;
let the Fathers applaud!
I enter upon the Gāyatrī meter;
I enter upon the Triṣṭubh meter;
I enter upon the Jagatī meter;
I enter upon the Anuṣṭubh meter;
I enter upon the Paṅkti meter;
I enter upon the meters as meter.
May they aid us;
may they protect us;
may she reach him who hates us and whom we hate.
O Brahman, we shall proceed with the Pravargya;
O Hotṛ, praise the Gharma;
O Āgnīdhra, set on the fire the two Rajanarohiṇa cakes;
O Pratiprasthātṛ, distribute [the fires];
O Prastotṛ, sing the Sāmans!
Joined with the Yajus, accompanied by the Sāmans, shaped by the Ṛc, brought together by all inspired thoughts, with sacrificial gifts,
extended, conveying across, let the stobhas, being well-disposed, carry [it].
Bhūr, Bhuvaḥ, Svar!
At the impulse of the god Savitṛ, impelled by Bṛhaspati.
Om! Possessed of Indra, proceed!

4.9.5.m:
Ten [regions] to the east, ten to the south thou shinest;
ten to the west, ten to the north thou shinest;
ten upward thou shinest, being well-minded.
As such, protect our offspring and livestock, without malice.
Let Agni with the Vasus make thee shine from the east;
shining thus, make me shine.
Let the Fathers having Yama as king, with the Fathers, make thee shine from the south;
shining thus, make me shine.
Let Savitṛ with the Ādityas make thee shine from the west;
shining thus, make me shine.
Let Mitra and Varuṇa with the Maruts make thee shine from the north;
shining thus, make me shine.
Let Bṛhaspati with all the gods make thee shine from above;
shining thus, make me shine.
O divine Gharma, shining art thou among the gods;
make me shining well among gods and men;
place radiance in me.
Thou art radiance;
thou art the radiant one.
As thou shinest with radiance, so may I shine with radiance;
may the radiances of me as I shine be like thine.
Thou art speed;
thou art the speedy one.
As thou speedest with speed, so may I speed with speed;
may the speeds of me as I speed be like thine.
Thou art splendor;
thou art the splendid one.
As thou splendorest with splendor, so may I splendor with splendor;
may the splendors of me as I splendor be like thine.

4.9.10.m:
Thou art the light of the cattle distributed among the gods;
thou art the light, the sheen.
The sap of the trees, of the waters, of the plants;
thee, who art spirited, I pour down into the spirited one;
upward is the mind, winning heaven.
Thou art milk;
may I be full of milk.
O Gharma, what heat of thine is in heaven, what is in heaven, what is in the high [region], what is in thunder, what is in the Jagatī meter—that [heat] of thine I avert with this [offering];
to that of thine, svāhā!
O Gharma, what heat of thine is in the intermediate space, what is in the intermediate space, what is in the wind, what is in the Vāmadevya [sāman], what is in the Triṣṭubh meter—that [heat] of thine I avert with this [offering];
to that of thine, svāhā!
O Gharma, what heat of thine is in the earth, what is in the earth, what is in fire, what is in the Rathantara [sāman], what is in the Gāyatrī meter—that [heat] of thine I avert with this [offering];
to that of thine, svāhā!
O Prastotṛ, sing the sāman!
"Along with us today"—this is [the verse].
To protect the priesthood from afar, protect the body of princely power!
By the ordinance of the clan, we stride around thee for newer welfare.
O Prastotṛ, sing the sāman!
"Along with us today"—this is [the verse].
To protect the breath from afar, protect the body of sight!
By the ordinance of hearing, we stride around thee for newer welfare.
O Prastotṛ, sing the sāman!
"Along with us today"—this is [the verse].
To protect heaven from afar, protect the body of the intermediate space!
By the ordinance of the earth, we stride around thee for newer welfare.
Four-cornered, the navel of truth, the seat, the Gharma, containing all life-spans, protection extensive;
we have kept off hatred, kept off the crookedness of him whose vow is alien.
Fair art thou, bestowing welfare and blessing;
a child suckling the folk.
Bring both welfare,
and carry [us] around.
O Gharma, this is thy food, this thy debris;
by that grow and swell;
and may we grow and may we swell!
Confounded be he who hates us and whom we hate!
